<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- W3C SCXML 6.4.3: what an `<invoke>` `<param>` puts in the invoked
     session's data model, and what it must not.

     Two sentences of that section govern this fixture, and each one has
     a phase:

       "If the 'name' of a <param> element in the <invoke> matches the
        'id' of a <data> element in the top-level data declarations of
        the invoked session, the SCXML Processor MUST use the value of
        the <param> element as the initial value of the corresponding
        <data> element."

       "If the names do not match, the Processor MUST NOT add the value
        of the <param> element or namelist key/value pair to the invoked
        session's data model."

     The first sentence says VALUE. A processor that hands the child the
     author's expression TEXT and lets the child's engine evaluate it
     answers a different question — "what does this source mean in the
     child?" — and the two questions only agree while the expression is
     a literal. The whole W3C IRP param/namelist surface is literals
     (226, 240, 241, 243, 244, 245, 276 all pass `1`), so a suite that
     is green says nothing about which session evaluated: `1` is `1` in
     either data model. This fixture makes the two answers differ.

       shadowed    the child declares `token` too, with its own value.
                   `<param name="seen" expr="token"/>` must carry the
                   PARENT's `token`. A child-side evaluation finds the
                   child's and reports it — a wrong value with no error
                   anywhere, which is why nothing caught this.

       soleName    `<param name="seen" expr="only_here"/>` names a
                   variable only the parent has. A child-side evaluation
                   cannot resolve it at all, so this phase separates
                   "evaluated in the wrong session and found a shadow"
                   from "evaluated in the wrong session and found
                   nothing".

       unmatched   two params, one naming a `<data>` the child declares
                   and one naming nothing. The declared one must arrive;
                   the other must not become a variable in the child.
                   The child reports `typeof` on it, so a leak is named
                   rather than inferred from an absence.

       namelist    the same rule for the sibling syntax §scxml-6.4.1
                   spells as "the value stored at the location is the
                   value", carrying a STRING so that a processor which
                   forwards the rendered text as an expression is caught
                   doing an identifier lookup in the child.

       infinite    a non-finite number, which the data model holds and no
                   Lua grammar can spell. A channel that renders the
                   value as source for the child to re-read loses the
                   pair outright; one that passes the value delivers it.

     Every child answers with an EVENT of its own rather than through
     `<donedata>`, for the reason docs/SCE_INTEGRATION_FIXTURE_LAYOUT.md
     states as a rule: fixtures stay on one axis. A `<donedata>` verdict
     would make a regression in the donedata lift — which
     `donedata_local_invoke` owns — arrive here disguised as a param
     seeding failure. It also decides the question in the wrong place:
     the child is where the seeded value lives, so the child is what
     should compare it, and a bare event name carries that answer with
     no payload path in between.

     The vocabulary is one set of four names, reused by every phase, and
     the parent's transitions in each phase say what they mean there:

       seed.ok        the child saw exactly the invoking session's value
       seed.shadowed  the child saw its OWN same-named variable instead
       seed.missing   the child saw neither — its `<data>` default stood
       seed.leaked    a name the child never declared became a variable
       seed.collapsed the value arrived as a different type than it was

     Each phase lands its failure in a `<final>` of its own, so a
     regression says which sentence broke rather than "param passing
     failed".

     This fixture is the canonical source for all 7 channels — see
     docs/SCE_INTEGRATION_FIXTURE_LAYOUT.md for the per-backend test
     locations and engine-channel coverage (Interpreter / AOT).
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml" version="1.0"
       datamodel="ecmascript" initial="shadowed"
       name="invoke_param_seeds_declared_child_data">
    <datamodel>
        <data id="token" expr="'parent'"/>
        <data id="only_here" expr="'sole'"/>
    </datamodel>

    <state id="shadowed">
        <invoke type="scxml" id="inv_shadow">
            <param name="seen" expr="token"/>
            <content>
                <scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml" version="1.0"
                       datamodel="ecmascript" initial="report">
                    <datamodel>
                        <data id="seen" expr="'unset'"/>
                        <data id="token" expr="'child'"/>
                    </datamodel>
                    <state id="report">
                        <transition cond="seen === 'parent'" target="done">
                            <send target="#_parent" event="seed.ok"/>
                        </transition>
                        <transition cond="seen === 'child'" target="done">
                            <send target="#_parent" event="seed.shadowed"/>
                        </transition>
                        <transition target="done">
                            <send target="#_parent" event="seed.missing"/>
                        </transition>
                    </state>
                    <final id="done"/>
                </scxml>
            </content>
        </invoke>
        <transition event="seed.ok" target="soleName"/>
        <transition event="seed.shadowed" target="failChildEvaluatedTheExpression"/>
        <transition event="seed.missing" target="failShadowSeedLost"/>
        <transition event="error.execution" target="failShadowSeedLost"/>
    </state>

    <state id="soleName">
        <invoke type="scxml" id="inv_sole">
            <param name="seen" expr="only_here"/>
            <content>
                <scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml" version="1.0"
                       datamodel="ecmascript" initial="report">
                    <datamodel>
                        <data id="seen" expr="'unset'"/>
                    </datamodel>
                    <state id="report">
                        <transition cond="seen === 'sole'" target="done">
                            <send target="#_parent" event="seed.ok"/>
                        </transition>
                        <transition target="done">
                            <send target="#_parent" event="seed.missing"/>
                        </transition>
                    </state>
                    <final id="done"/>
                </scxml>
            </content>
        </invoke>
        <transition event="seed.ok" target="unmatched"/>
        <transition event="seed.missing" target="failParentOnlyExprLost"/>
        <transition event="error.execution" target="failParentOnlyExprLost"/>
    </state>

    <state id="unmatched">
        <invoke type="scxml" id="inv_unmatched">
            <param name="declared" expr="'carried'"/>
            <param name="nowhere" expr="'leaked'"/>
            <content>
                <scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml" version="1.0"
                       datamodel="ecmascript" initial="report">
                    <datamodel>
                        <data id="declared" expr="'unset'"/>
                    </datamodel>
                    <state id="report">
                        <transition cond="typeof nowhere !== 'undefined'" target="done">
                            <send target="#_parent" event="seed.leaked"/>
                        </transition>
                        <transition cond="declared === 'carried'" target="done">
                            <send target="#_parent" event="seed.ok"/>
                        </transition>
                        <transition target="done">
                            <send target="#_parent" event="seed.missing"/>
                        </transition>
                    </state>
                    <final id="done"/>
                </scxml>
            </content>
        </invoke>
        <transition event="seed.ok" target="namelistPhase"/>
        <transition event="seed.leaked" target="failUnmatchedParamEnteredTheChild"/>
        <transition event="seed.missing" target="failDeclaredParamLost"/>
        <transition event="error.execution" target="failDeclaredParamLost"/>
    </state>

    <!-- §scxml-6.4.1 names the same rule for the sibling syntax: "the name
         of the location is the attribute and THE VALUE STORED AT THE
         LOCATION is the value". `namelist` may not occur alongside
         `<param>` on one `<invoke>` (§scxml-6.4.1 attribute constraints),
         so it needs its own.

         The parent's `token` is a STRING here, which is what makes this
         phase see anything: a processor that renders the value and hands
         the child that text as an EXPRESSION turns 'parent' into an
         identifier lookup in the child's data model, where no such name
         exists. The W3C corpus passes numbers through namelist (240, 241,
         244, 245), and `1` renders to `1`, which evaluates to `1` — the
         defect is invisible to every one of them. -->
    <state id="namelistPhase">
        <invoke type="scxml" id="inv_namelist" namelist="token">
            <content>
                <scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml" version="1.0"
                       datamodel="ecmascript" initial="report">
                    <datamodel>
                        <data id="token" expr="'unset'"/>
                    </datamodel>
                    <state id="report">
                        <transition cond="token === 'parent'" target="done">
                            <send target="#_parent" event="seed.ok"/>
                        </transition>
                        <transition target="done">
                            <send target="#_parent" event="seed.missing"/>
                        </transition>
                    </state>
                    <final id="done"/>
                </scxml>
            </content>
        </invoke>
        <transition event="seed.ok" target="infinite"/>
        <transition event="seed.missing" target="failNamelistValueLost"/>
        <transition event="error.execution" target="failNamelistValueLost"/>
    </state>

    <!-- §scxml-6.4.3 again, on the value a processor can least afford to
         re-render: a non-finite number. `1/0` is Infinity under §scxml-B-2's
         ECMAScript semantics, and it is a value the data model holds
         perfectly well — every channel that hands the child the VALUE
         delivers it.

         A channel that renders the value as engine SOURCE for the child to
         re-read has to spell Infinity in that engine's grammar, and Lua has
         no spelling for it: the renderers produce `inf` (Rust's `{}`) and
         `+Inf` (Go's `%g`), neither of which is a Lua expression. The child
         then fails to evaluate what it was handed and the pair is dropped
         entirely — the same hole this repository already closed on the WIRE
         side, where `ScriptResultUtils::resultToString` reached
         `static_cast<int64_t>(inf)`.

         So this phase is what separates "passes the value" from "passes
         source that happens to parse back for every value a test tried". -->
    <state id="infinite">
        <invoke type="scxml" id="inv_infinite">
            <param name="seen" expr="1/0"/>
            <content>
                <scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml" version="1.0"
                       datamodel="ecmascript" initial="report">
                    <datamodel>
                        <data id="seen" expr="'unset'"/>
                    </datamodel>
                    <state id="report">
                        <transition cond="typeof seen === 'number' &amp;&amp; seen &gt; 1e308" target="done">
                            <send target="#_parent" event="seed.ok"/>
                        </transition>
                        <transition cond="typeof seen === 'string'" target="done">
                            <send target="#_parent" event="seed.missing"/>
                        </transition>
                        <transition target="done">
                            <send target="#_parent" event="seed.collapsed"/>
                        </transition>
                    </state>
                    <final id="done"/>
                </scxml>
            </content>
        </invoke>
        <transition event="seed.ok" target="pass"/>
        <transition event="seed.missing" target="failInfiniteParamLost"/>
        <transition event="seed.collapsed" target="failInfiniteParamCollapsed"/>
        <transition event="error.execution" target="failInfiniteParamLost"/>
    </state>

    <final id="pass"/>
    <final id="failChildEvaluatedTheExpression"/>
    <final id="failShadowSeedLost"/>
    <final id="failParentOnlyExprLost"/>
    <final id="failUnmatchedParamEnteredTheChild"/>
    <final id="failDeclaredParamLost"/>
    <final id="failNamelistValueLost"/>
    <final id="failInfiniteParamLost"/>
    <final id="failInfiniteParamCollapsed"/>
</scxml>
